  │     │ [3mchanged, when, by whom[0m                          │ from day one, expensive to retrofit                     │
  ├─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [1mD[0m   │ [1mSubscriptions[0m — what "tasks they care about"    │ Pure product question, independent of delivery          │
  │     │ resolves to                                     │                                                         │
  ├─────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [1mE[0m   │ [1mDelivery[0m — in-app bell, email, push, digest     │ The part you asked for                                  │
  └─────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  C is the interesting one. If A is built without it, adding notifications later means reconstructing history that was
  never recorded — so the notification goal changes how we build the [3mfirst[0m piece, even though delivery ships last.

  ---

  [1mThe fork that decides all of it:[0m whether other people can change your tasks.

  - [1mSingle-user, local[0m — tasks live in the browser (localStorage), no server, no accounts. "Notifications" then only
    meaningfully covers [3mtime[0m: due-date and reminder alerts, since you already know about every change you made yourself.
    Cheap: days, no infrastructure. Rules out collaboration without a rewrite of the storage layer.
  - [1mMulti-user, shared[0m — real backend, accounts, a database, a change log. "Someone else edited a task you're watching"
    becomes meaningful, which is what your phrasing implies. Expensive: this is the bulk of the work, and it's the piece
    that's genuinely hard to change later.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user-shaped[0m — build local-only, but with a server-ready data model and a real change-event
    log from the start. Costs maybe 20% extra now to avoid a rewrite later. You still only get time-based notifications
    until a backend exists.

  Cheap to change later: which channels you deliver on, what the digest schedule is, how the bell UI looks. Expensive to
  change later: storage location, whether events are recorded, whether identity exists.

  [1mMy recommendation is the third option.[0m Your phrasing ("users… tasks they care about") reads multi-user to me, but
  committing to auth and a backend before a single task can be created is a lot of infrastructure spent ahead of a
  product you haven't validated. Building local-first with an event log and a user field keeps the notification design
  honest without paying for servers on day one.

